course: dictionary
lesson: 10961
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so you cannot always guess from the word alone. Context, the words and sentences around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants. Wally is a good example: it can be a silly person, a Scottish ornament, or a name.
explain: «He laughed out loud, because he would feel like a giant wally.» The words «feel» and «giant» show that wally means a silly, foolish person, and the writer is describing how embarrassed he would be. Try the meaning in the sentence: «feel like a giant silly person» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: In UK playgrounds, «you wally!» is a gentle way of calling someone silly, often in fun. It is much milder than idiot. Ask yourself: is the sentence about a person being silly, a thing in a house, or a name?
fact: Some words have more than one meaning. Context tells you which one is used.
fact: «Wally» most often means a silly or foolish person, said in a mild, often friendly way.
fact: In Scotland, a «wally dug» is a china dog ornament, and a «wally close» is a tiled entrance.
fact: «Wally» is also a name, as in the puzzle books «Where's Wally?», which are called «Where's Waldo?» in the USA and Canada.
fact: «Wally» is a milder insult than «idiot», so it is often said in fun.
exercise: «He laughed out loud, because he would feel like a giant wally.» What does wally mean here? | A tiled entrance | *A silly or foolish person | A china dog ornament | A striped jumper
exercise: In the puzzle books, you search crowded pictures for Wally in his red-and-white striped jumper. In this sentence, wally is | A silly person | A china ornament | *A name | A tiled entrance
exercise: Ellie's gran has a «wally dug» on her windowsill. What is a wally dug? | *A china dog ornament | A puzzle book | A tiled entrance to a house | A silly person
test: Lewis says «you wally!» with a grin after he drops his cup. What is he doing? | Pointing at a puzzle picture | Giving a very serious insult, much worse than idiot | Naming a tiled entrance | *Gently calling his friend silly, in fun
test: In Scotland, what is a «wally close»? | A striped jumper | *A tiled entrance | A name in a puzzle book | A silly person
exam: Which is the best way to work out which meaning of wally is used in a sentence? | Guess at random without reading the sentence | *Read the words around it and check which meaning fits | Always choose the first meaning you see | Skip the word and never think about it again
